there's a fascinating new chart from the  new york times and the mit media lab and  this compares coveted cases per 100  people meaning per capita coveted to how  people voted in the 2020 presidential  election and the results are so simple  that it's almost surprising but then  once you think about it once you look at  it you realize actually it's not really  that surprising and the bottom line of  the findings are as follows  if there are lots of trump voters  there's lots of covid right now over the  last month if there's a medium amount of  trump voters there's a medium amount of  covet over the last month and if there  aren't that many trump uh voters there's  really not that much covid spreading  either so let's take a look at the image  and very very nicely put together so  what you're seeing here are five  separate lines which track new cases per  one hundred thousand  so  the blue line means there are under  twenty percent trump voters in the  county this is a county by county thing  if you're not watching i'm going to  describe this to you and then it goes  from the blue line all the way up to the  bright red line that means more than 80  percent of a county has trump voters now  remember that if you go back to the  beginning before we knew much about  covid  early covid hit the places with the big  airports that are directly connected to  asia and europe and those are mostly in  blue states so early on at the start of  the pandemic and you look to the left  side of the chart in april you saw that  it was blue counties with more covid  because we had this airport-based  initial spread you had new york city you  had boston new york airport over over  the water in jersey san francisco  eventually la et cetera  now after a year and a half of the  pandemic having vaccines understanding  guidelines you've essentially seen  that since roughly august or september  of 2020 it's been the red counties that  on a per capita basis are leading the  covid case rates the more red the county  the more they are leading in terms of  cases and that's not good  the lowest new case rates right now  are in the counties with fewer than 20  percent of the population less than 20  percent of the population fewer than 20  trump voters those are the places with  the least covet  followed by the places with 20 to 40  percent trump  and then the places with 40 to 60  percent trump voters have more coveted  and then the places with 60 to 80  percent trump have more covet and the  places over the last month with the  highest per capita coveted case rates  are the counties where more than 80  percent of the population voted for  donald trump and it's not by a small  margin if you look at those  20 or or or lower percent trump counties  they only have 25 new cases per 100 000  if you look at the 80 plus percent trump  counties they have 65 new cases per 100  000 in other words the big trump  counties  have about two and a half just shy of  three times the new case rate than do  the very democratic leading counties  it's truly incredible to see this  translate  perfectly into virus spread now we  expected this intuitively we expected  this  but the data shows it cleanly and  clearly now you might correctly say  this can't be good for winning elections  right like in other words if it's mostly  your voters who are getting sick  and overwhelmingly your voters that are  going to the hospital and overwhelmingly  your voters that are dying that probably  isn't good for winning elections right  and you would be completely correct  except republicans aren't actually  trying to win  by having more people who agree with  them go out and vote republicans for a  long time and recently and into the  future are trying to win by planning to  suppress the vote and making it harder  for people unlikely to vote for them to  actually be able to get a vote counted  not by winning people over on policy  ideas that being said let's go back to  the discussion that we had 